BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Lake Champlain Memory Café
DTSTART:20261021T150000
DTEND:20261021T160000
DTSTAMP:20261021T155930Z
UID:91057c409cdec49409d72fcab107a0@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:The Lake Champlain Memory Café offers an inviting space and t
 ime for those living with dementia – and their care partners/families. C
 are partners have an opportunity to socialize with other caregivers while 
 their loved ones enjoy a program at the New North End Library. The Café i
 s a partnership between FFL\, the Center on Aging at The University of Ver
 mont\, and Age Well. This month's activity is a mini-performance and Q&A w
 ith a UVM student chamber ensemble.\n\nSources: Vermont Public
LOCATION:Fletcher Free Library\, New North End\, 1127 North Ave
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/lake-champ
 lain-memory-cafe-16-09-2026-00-24-09
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Midweek Organ Concert
DTSTART:20261021T121500
DTEND:20261021T130000
DTSTAMP:20261021T155930Z
UID:69b85df36eafba2958de11ec666930@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:Robert Ludwig will present a program of music from the 18th to
  the 21st centuries for the pipe organ on the college’s 50-rank\, 3100 p
 ipe Gress-Miles instrument. The centerpiece of the program is J.S. Bach’
 s complex masterpiece Toccata and Fugue in d (Dorian)\, BWV 538. The progr
 am also includes compositions by S.S. Wesley\, Edward Elgar\, and Elfrida 
 Andrée\, an activist in the Swedish women’s movement and the first Swed
 ish woman to conduct a symphony orchestra. The program concludes with an a
 rrangement of We Shall Overcome by Carl Haywood\, Conductor of the Nationa
 l HBCU Concert Choir. Ludwig is Director of Music at St. Stephen’s Episc
 opal Church on the Green in Middlebury. Now retired from full-time work\, 
 his career included more than two decades of directing the music programs 
 of two notable cathedrals\, conducting dozens of premieres of commissioned
  pieces\, conducting concerts throughout the East Coast\, Canada and the U
 .K.\, serving on the Fulbright Screening Committee for Organ and Harpsicho
 rd\, and teaching vocal music and music theory at one of the top high scho
 ols in the U.S. for music education.\n\nSources: Vermont Public
LOCATION:Middlebury Chapel\, 75 Hepburn Road
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/midweek-or
 gan-concert-30-09-2026-15-09-22
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Unveiling the Shadows: Myths and Legends of the NEK and North Coun
 try w/ Robert Jones
DTSTART:20261021T190000
DTEND:20261021T200000
DTSTAMP:20261021T155930Z
UID:8a0f0cf592d9b258d569d1dd3a95f2@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:The Northeast Kingdom and the New Hampshire North Country are 
 lands of deep woods\, granite peaks\, and long winters—the perfect breed
 ing ground for stories that refuse to die. Join us for an evocative journe
 y into the folklore that defines our rugged corner of New England. From th
 e elusive Lake Memphremagog sea serpent to the whispered tales of Abenaki 
 legends\, forgotten ghost towns\, and the strange occurrences that haunt t
 he White Mountains\, we will explore how these myths shaped the identity o
 f the people who call this wilderness home. Whether you're a skeptic\, a b
 eliever\, or simply a lover of a good yarn\, this talk will peel back the 
 layers of local history to reveal the magic and mystery hidden in plain si
 ght. Presented by Robert Jones\, Owner of St. Johnsbury’s Ysbrydoliaeth 
 Curiosity Shop / Magasin de curiosités & NEK Language School. This event 
 is free\, ADA accessible\, and open to the public.\n\nSources: Vermont Pub
 lic
LOCATION:St. Johnsbury Athenaeum\, 1171 Main St.
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/unveiling-
 the-shadows-myths-and-legends-of-the-nek-and-north-country-w-robert-jones-
 05-10-2026-17-01-45
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Wednesdays with Farmer Fred in Plymouth Notch
DTSTART:20261021T120000
DTEND:20261021T130000
DTSTAMP:20261021T155930Z
UID:9938b164b9eef8937e884af4d6757e@vermont-events
DESCRIPTION:The Calvin Coolidge State Historic Site’s resident farmer wi
 ll be on hand each Wednesday from June 10 to October 21 to demonstrate his
 toric farming tools and techniques. Visitors may witness such activities a
 s sheep shearing\, rye harvesting\, or cider pressing. In addition to bein
 g an excellent educator\, Farmer Fred is a renowned storyteller and is sur
 e to entertain. Calvin Coolidge State Historic Site Every week through Oct
  21\, 2026.\n\nSources: Vermont Public
LOCATION:Vermont Public is independent\, community-supported media\, servi
 ng Vermont with trusted\, relevant and essential information. We share sto
 ries that bring people together\, from every corner of our region.\, © 20
 26 Vermont Public | 365 Troy Ave. Colchester\, VT 05446
URL:https://www.vermontpublic.org/vermont-events-calendar/event/wednesdays
 -with-farmer-fred-in-plymouth-notch-18-05-2026-11-16-39
END:VEVENT
